# known gap (todo/t0942) — SELF-HOST lane UNDER-rejects D4/D12 drop-taint at a
# field sub-place of a GENERIC struct.
#
# `h.r` is a live drop-tainted field place at a collection put — position 3 of
# the D12 ownership boundary set. The Rust lane rejects it with
# E_MoveWithoutOperator; the self-host ACCEPTS it and emits C.
#
# Mechanism: the self-host's D12 gate `reject_tainted_place`
# (self_host_typechecker/typecheck.gg) types the place with `infer_expr_type`,
# whose `EFieldAccess` arm has cases for `RTDefined`, `RTPrimitive` and `RTTuple`
# but NONE for `RTGeneric` (self_host_typechecker/infer.gg). A generic-struct
# object therefore falls through to NO_TYPE and `is_drop_tainted_type` answers
# false. The Rust twin resolves the same place structurally
# (`tainted_place_name` -> `lvalue_value_type`,
# src/semantic/safety/check_expr.rs), and the self-host's OWN structural resolver
# does have the `RTGeneric` arm — which is why the sibling D53 gate
# (`reject_single_owner_init`, now on the structural resolver) rejects the
# unique-lock analogue at `d53_unique_lock/mutex_generic_struct_field_push_reject.gg`
# while this D12 one still slips through.
#
# The equivalent CONCRETE-struct shape rejects correctly
# (`d12_drop_purity/pos3_field_place_reject.gg`), so the discriminating axis is
# the object's ResolvedType being `RTGeneric`, not the position or the taint.
#
# This is the under-rejection class D12 exists to close: a bare copy of a live
# drop-tainted place gives two owners of one resource and the drop runs twice.
#
# INTENDED behaviour (what the `#[ignore]`d test asserts): rejected, with
# `cannot copy` + the codespan box, and NO C on stdout.
struct R:
    int id

equip R with Drop:
    void drop(!self):
        print("bye")

struct Holder[T]:
    R r
    T payload

void main():
    Holder[int] h = Holder[int](R(1), 2)
    Vector[R] v = Vector[R]()
    v.push(h.r)
    print(v.len())
